z2h = z2f (u6x + CASH) + an exact bytecode ledger, so that u6x + CASH replays u6x's games exactly until a rescue acts.
Why: z2f was not silent. Its keepout A game vs araid left u6x's at round 525, though no rescue could start (u6x's bank
never under 250 there). u6x spends whatever a turn leaves on Flow.work(FLOW_RESERVE), and other budget checks read the
clock too, so CASH's few hundred bytecodes a turn moved flow work between turns and the games drifted. Every "silent"
candidate so far drifted the same way.
The ledger: G.debt = the bytecodes this turn spent on code u6x does not run, minus 2; every budget read adds it back.
- Flow/Seal/KPath/BitFlow left(): Clock.getBytecodesLeft() + G.debt (the same 4 instructions as u6x's
  unlimited ? MAX : Clock.getBytecodesLeft() on the false branch, with the clock read 2 instructions earlier: the -2).
- The direct reads (King floor and END_KING_LEFT, Baby THROW_BC, SENSE2_LEFT, SWEEP_LEFT):
  Clock.getBytecodesLeft() + (G.debt += 8) - 6 (8 extra instructions, counted as they run).
- Each CASH hook in Baby.turn and moveDecision sits between two G.bc() reads (Clock.getBytecodeNum() in a method of
  its own: the engine counts a basic block only at its end, a Clock read is no block end, and a call of our own code
  is one, so the count read does not depend on javac's line labels), and the debt grows by their difference plus the
  hook's instructions outside them (11, 9, 11; checked in javap against the engine's instrumenter).
- Debug.mark reads u6x's count (getBytecodeNum() - (G.debt += 8) + 6): its hot-stage branch costs bytecodes.
- cashHeard/cashBan start at 0, not -100: no <clinit> cost (0 is safe in both uses).
- G.debt = 0 before G.init (its own 2 instructions) and -2 at the end of each turn, after Debug.mark(99) and before the replay tag (the next turn starts at 0 extra).
The indicator, stats and replay tag after Flow.work see the raw clock; they change no game state (the tag's pad keeps
its value mod 128).
Validation: z2h vs araid keepout A (seed 0) matches u6x's game state (turns.tsv, every robot's position, facing,
health, cheese and cooldowns) for all 2000 rounds (replay_eq.py; the same check finds z2f's divergence at r525), and
every one of our 74,340 turns reports the same hot= reading (costliest stage segment and its bytecodes) as u6x's
(hot_eq.py): the ledger is exact, not just lucky. Three earlier builds drifted at r950-r1050 (TRAINING_LOG 09:42, 09:55).
Mechanism evidence: bots/z2e/SNAPSHOT (chain cq2 on u9p: won 7 of 8 to u9p's 2).
Falsifiers:
- Screen vs zc6 (u6x's 48 cells, seed 0) with the s6 gates (t <= -1.5; per-round picks z <= -2; transfer per round
  < 0.97 in the gate's form; any overrun or exception). Replay check: a cell whose game differs from zc6's must show a
  rescue (kingsFormed above u6x's, or an SQ_CASH squeak in the dump when dumped); 2 or more cells differing with no
  rescue means the ledger leaks, and z2h is withdrawn as not silent.
- Replica: standard block rules (sets 3, 1, 2, 4; blocks 1/2/3 reject at pooled t <= 0/1/1.5; t >= 2 accepts from
  block 2; block 4 rejects below 2) with identity-aware scoring (--crown), and the per-block transfer floor.
